1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:13,973
In episode seven we used jal and jr ra, but RISC-V really has only two unconditional jump instructions.

2
00:00:13,973 --> 00:00:21,823
The jal instruction takes rd and a label, and its name means jump and link, but it actually links first,

3
00:00:21,823 --> 00:00:26,566
writing PC plus four into rd, and then jumps to the label.

4
00:00:26,566 --> 00:00:36,230
The jalr instruction, which takes rd, rs1, and an immediate, also links first, but it jumps to rs1 plus the immediate,

5
00:00:36,230 --> 00:00:39,633
so its address comes from a register.

6
00:00:39,633 --> 00:00:42,955
Every other jump is a pseudo-instruction.

7
00:00:42,955 --> 00:00:50,003
The j, jr, and ret instructions write the return address into x0, which throws it away,

8
00:00:50,003 --> 00:00:54,366
while a jal with no rd links into ra.

9
00:00:54,366 --> 00:00:56,150
So why two instructions?

10
00:00:56,150 --> 00:01:02,392
The target of jal is fixed at assembly time, which suits calling a function by name.

11
00:01:02,392 --> 00:01:11,162
The target of jalr is given by a register at run time, so it can return to the address in ra, call a function pointer,

12
00:01:11,162 --> 00:01:15,300
and, together with auipc, jump very far.

13
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:18,189
The star of this episode is the factorial.

14
00:01:18,189 --> 00:01:24,900
The factorial of one is one, and the factorial of n is n times the factorial of n minus one.

15
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:27,244
What happens if we save nothing?

16
00:01:27,244 --> 00:01:33,764
Suppose main calls factorial of two, so a0 is two, and ra remembers the way back to main.

17
00:01:33,764 --> 00:01:38,673
Then factorial of two puts the argument one into a0 and does a jal,

18
00:01:38,673 --> 00:01:42,933
which changes ra to point back into factorial of two.

19
00:01:42,933 --> 00:01:50,515
When factorial of one returns one, the two that factorial of two needed was long ago overwritten by the argument one.

20
00:01:50,515 --> 00:01:57,266
Worse, ra still points into factorial of two itself, so we can never get back to main.

21
00:01:57,266 --> 00:02:04,038
The easiest fix is to push all of x1 through x31 on every call, and pull them all back afterwards.

22
00:02:04,038 --> 00:02:10,466
But memory is slow, and a call really needs to protect only a few registers.

23
00:02:10,466 --> 00:02:14,300
The solution is the calling convention from episode seven.

24
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:18,927
The caller can count only on the s registers and sp staying unchanged.

25
00:02:18,927 --> 00:02:26,300
A callee that wants to use an s register has to save the old value first, and restore it before returning.

26
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:28,460
A recursive function wears two hats.

27
00:02:28,460 --> 00:02:35,133
To the level above it, it is a callee, and to the level below it, it is a caller.

28
00:02:35,133 --> 00:02:42,198
Here is the factorial from the notes, which follows the calling convention, starting at address 0x2000.

29
00:02:42,198 --> 00:02:46,066
First let's pick out the prologue and the epilogue.

30
00:02:46,066 --> 00:02:52,253
The prologue carves eight bytes out of the stack, which is the stack frame for this call.

31
00:02:52,253 --> 00:02:59,970
We need it because the later jal will overwrite ra, and s0, which we want to borrow, needs its old value saved.

32
00:02:59,970 --> 00:03:04,975
Then mv s0 a0 puts n into s0, and because the s registers survive calls,

33
00:03:04,975 --> 00:03:08,733
n is still there when the recursive call returns.

34
00:03:08,733 --> 00:03:16,691
The bne instruction can compare only two registers, so first we use li to put one into t0, and if n is not one,

35
00:03:16,691 --> 00:03:18,196
we branch to recurse.

36
00:03:18,196 --> 00:03:21,566
Otherwise it is the base case, where a0 is one.

37
00:03:21,566 --> 00:03:28,878
We can't simply do jr ra here, because s0 hasn't been restored and the stack frame hasn't been popped,

38
00:03:28,878 --> 00:03:31,666
so we use j to go to the epilogue.

39
00:03:31,666 --> 00:03:40,340
At recurse, a0 becomes n minus one, we call ourselves, and when we come back, a0 holds the factorial of n minus one,

40
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:42,808
which we multiply by the n in s0.

41
00:03:42,808 --> 00:03:46,920
The epilogue runs in the reverse order of the prologue.

42
00:03:46,920 --> 00:03:56,300
So we first get ra and s0 back from the stack, then add eight to sp to pop the frame, and finally do jr ra.

43
00:03:56,300 --> 00:03:59,806
Let's trace the factorial of three from start to finish.

44
00:03:59,806 --> 00:04:05,300
Main has already set aside a twelve-byte stack frame, and sp points just below it.

45
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:16,866
Main puts three into a0 and executes the jal at 0x1004, which makes ra equal 0x1008 and jumps into factorial.

46
00:04:16,866 --> 00:04:25,215
In the prologue for the factorial of three, sp drops by eight, and then ra, which is 0x1008, and main's s0,

47
00:04:25,215 --> 00:04:27,933
which is forty-two, are saved.

48
00:04:27,933 --> 00:04:30,241
The mv sets s0 to three.

49
00:04:30,241 --> 00:04:41,100
Since three is not one, we branch to recurse and set a0 to two, and then the jal at 0x2024 sets ra to 0x2028.

50
00:04:41,100 --> 00:04:49,827
The factorial of two pushes its own frame in the same way, saving ra, which is 0x2028, and s0, which is three.

51
00:04:49,827 --> 00:04:54,000
Then it calls itself again with a0 equal to one.

52
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:02,133
The factorial of one pushes one more frame, saving ra, which is 0x2028, and s0, which is two.

53
00:05:02,133 --> 00:05:06,382
Each of the three frames holds its own copy of ra and s0.

54
00:05:06,382 --> 00:05:15,333
The ra saved by the factorial of three leads back to main, while the other two both lead back to the mul at 0x2028.

55
00:05:15,333 --> 00:05:22,113
The factorial of one is the base case, so a0 becomes one and we jump to the epilogue.

56
00:05:22,113 --> 00:05:29,929
The epilogue gets back ra, which is 0x2028, and s0, which is two, then sp returns to where it was,

57
00:05:29,929 --> 00:05:32,766
and jr ra goes back to the mul.

58
00:05:32,766 --> 00:05:37,272
Now a0 is s0 times a0, which is two times one, or two.

59
00:05:37,272 --> 00:05:41,861
The two in s0 is exactly the n of the factorial of two,

60
00:05:41,861 --> 00:05:48,066
because the factorial of one used s0 but restored it before returning.

61
00:05:48,066 --> 00:05:54,883
The factorial of two finishes its epilogue, getting back ra, s0, and sp, and returns to the mul,

62
00:05:54,883 --> 00:05:58,221
where a0 becomes three times two, which is six.

63
00:05:58,221 --> 00:06:05,535
Then the factorial of three gets back ra and main's s0, which is forty-two, sp returns to where it was,

64
00:06:05,535 --> 00:06:07,466
and we return to main.

65
00:06:07,466 --> 00:06:13,161
Back in main, a0 is six, and sp, like s0, is exactly what it was before the call.

66
00:06:13,161 --> 00:06:19,800
The popped stack frames are still in memory, but nobody uses them any more.

67
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:26,010
The foo function in the notes is recursive too, since foo of i is i plus foo of i minus one.

68
00:06:26,010 --> 00:06:31,208
Put its assembly next to the factorial, and the skeleton is exactly the same.

69
00:06:31,208 --> 00:06:32,895
Only three things differ.

70
00:06:32,895 --> 00:06:38,093
It compares directly with x0, which saves the li, the base case returns zero,

71
00:06:38,093 --> 00:06:41,366
and the multiplication becomes an addition.

72
00:06:41,366 --> 00:06:47,170
The notes' main puts foo of three into s0, and it is still there after calling foo of a hundred.

73
00:06:47,170 --> 00:06:54,666
At its deepest, foo of a hundred has a hundred and one stack frames, eight hundred eight bytes in all.

74
00:06:54,666 --> 00:06:56,969
Not every function needs a stack.

75
00:06:56,969 --> 00:07:02,341
In the notes, sum_square calls mult twice, and mult calls no function at all.

76
00:07:02,341 --> 00:07:09,040
A function like mult is called a leaf function, since it has no jal, so ra is never overwritten.

77
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:17,100
If it uses only temporary and argument registers, it needs no sw instruction and no lw instruction.

78
00:07:17,100 --> 00:07:20,377
Here is the complete table of register conventions.

79
00:07:20,377 --> 00:07:27,833
The gp register and the tp register have special purposes of their own and aren't covered by the calling convention,

80
00:07:27,833 --> 00:07:29,118
so don't touch them.

81
00:07:29,118 --> 00:07:35,095
And s0 is also called fp, the frame pointer, because sp may move in the middle of a function,

82
00:07:35,095 --> 00:07:39,466
while fp always points at the current stack frame.

83
00:07:39,466 --> 00:07:44,832
The func_a in the notes moves sp in the middle of the function, since it calls func_b,

84
00:07:44,832 --> 00:07:48,133
and it needs t1 both before and after the call.

85
00:07:48,133 --> 00:07:51,794
The prologue pushes eight bytes, saving ra and s0.

86
00:07:51,794 --> 00:07:58,166
Normally ra is the caller's to save, but by habit it is saved in the prologue too.

87
00:07:58,166 --> 00:08:00,488
Now t1 is ten and s0 is twenty.

88
00:08:00,488 --> 00:08:07,227
The t1 register is caller-saved, so func_b might change it, which is why, before the call,

89
00:08:07,227 --> 00:08:10,433
we push another four bytes to save t1.

90
00:08:10,433 --> 00:08:14,214
After func_b returns, we can't tell what is in t1 any more.

91
00:08:14,214 --> 00:08:21,433
Luckily there is a backup on the stack, so we get t1 back as ten, and pop those four bytes right away.

92
00:08:21,433 --> 00:08:24,528
Once sp moves, the offsets move with it.

93
00:08:24,528 --> 00:08:30,300
A moment ago zero of sp was t1, and only after the pop is it ra again.

94
00:08:30,300 --> 00:08:34,089
In the end, t1 is fifteen and s0 is twenty-five.

95
00:08:34,089 --> 00:08:43,066
The epilogue gets back ra and the caller's s0, which is forty-two, sp is restored, and ret returns.

96
00:08:43,066 --> 00:08:47,512
Here are three true-or-false questions, taken from the notes' exercises.

97
00:08:47,512 --> 00:08:51,600
Think about them for yourself first.

98
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:53,194
Statement one is false.

99
00:08:53,194 --> 00:09:00,265
The registers a0 and a1 carry back return values, and the argument registers, like the temporary ones,

100
00:09:00,265 --> 00:09:01,928
are saved by the caller.

101
00:09:01,928 --> 00:09:03,453
Statement two is true.

102
00:09:03,453 --> 00:09:10,333
The s registers are saved by the callee, usually in the prologue and restored in the epilogue.

103
00:09:10,333 --> 00:09:11,969
Statement three is false.

104
00:09:11,969 --> 00:09:16,288
The func_a function pushed t1 right in the middle of the function.

105
00:09:16,288 --> 00:09:22,233
As long as pushes and pops are paired, the stack can be used at any time.

106
00:09:22,233 --> 00:09:26,134
Finally, let's go over the six steps from episode seven again.

107
00:09:26,134 --> 00:09:28,587
Steps one and two belong to the caller.

108
00:09:28,587 --> 00:09:35,508
It saves any temporary or argument registers it still needs, puts the arguments in place, and then does a jal.

109
00:09:35,508 --> 00:09:41,359
Step three is the prologue, where sp is lowered only once, so local arrays are allocated too.

110
00:09:41,359 --> 00:09:46,500
We also save the s registers we will use, and ra if we call other functions.

111
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:48,747
Step four is the function body.

112
00:09:48,747 --> 00:09:57,376
Steps five and six are the epilogue, where we put the return value into a0, restore the registers, pop the stack frame,

113
00:09:57,376 --> 00:10:00,333
and finish with jr ra.

114
00:10:00,333 --> 00:10:05,203
The notes offer an analogy, where calling a function is like house-sitting for your parents.

115
00:10:05,203 --> 00:10:10,920
The parents are the caller and you are the callee, while the desk is the registers and the closet is memory.

116
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:13,726
What the parents leave on the desk are the arguments.

117
00:10:13,726 --> 00:10:18,278
Whatever else is on the desk, you first put away in the closet, which is the prologue.

118
00:10:18,278 --> 00:10:26,966
When you leave, you put it all back exactly as it was, and leave a gift, the return value, which is the epilogue.

